WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.840
"Barbie" had actors lining up to&nbsp;
play Kens, but it didn't work out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.860
for everyone for a surprising reason.&nbsp;
Who backed away from the part, and why?

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:10.800
It's likely not a huge surprise&nbsp;
that, when it was announced that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.280
Oscar-nominated writer-director Greta&nbsp;
Gerwig was making a "Barbie" movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:19.560
actors across Hollywood wanted to get involved&nbsp;
right away. "Vanity Fair" spoke to two of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.840
movie's casting directors, Lucy Bevan and Allison&nbsp;
Jones, to dig deeper into how the candy-coated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:29.340
fiercely feminist film managed to score&nbsp;
its star-studded lineup. As it turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:34.980
we missed out on three potential Kens — Bowen&nbsp;
Yang, Dan Levy, and Ben Platt all wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:39.840
play Kens, but couldn't make it work due to&nbsp;
COVID-19 restrictions in place during filming.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:43.320
"What do I have to do?"
"You have to go to the real world."

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:47.220
While "Barbie" was in production, the&nbsp;
entire cast had to commit to staying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:51.900
in London for the entirety of shoot, due&nbsp;
to strict COVID-19 protocols — and Yang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.440
Levy, and Platt couldn't make the&nbsp;
commitment, for a variety of reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:00.300
It's easy enough to guess why — Yang is a&nbsp;
cast member on "Saturday Night Live," Dan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.500
Levy just briefly worked on "The Idol,"&nbsp;
and Platt is currently starring in "Parade"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.940
on Broadway as well as the new film "Theater&nbsp;
Camp." Still, the casting directors said those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:12.780
actors were quite upset that they couldn't&nbsp;
make it work. Jones told "Vanity Fair,"

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.960
"They were, I'm not kidding,&nbsp;
really bummed they couldn't do it."

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:19.800
According to the "Vanity Fair" piece, Ken&nbsp;
wasn't the only part in "Barbie" being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.640
fought over in Hollywood. Incredibly,&nbsp;
the role of Allan — "Ken's buddy" who,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.660
in doll form, fits into all of Ken's&nbsp;
same clothes — was a hot ticket as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:31.680
and at first, Jonathan Groff, known for&nbsp;
projects like "Mindhunter," "Looking,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.000
and "Frozen," really, really wanted to&nbsp;
play the amorphous blob known as Allan.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.400
Unfortunately for Groff, he suffered the same fate&nbsp;
as the others. Jones isn't specific about whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:45.960
or not Groff was simply auditioning himself for&nbsp;
the part or actually in talks further down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:50.760
line, but she does say that, eventually, Groff&nbsp;
reached out and said he couldn't make London work.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:54.780
The role of Allan ultimately went to&nbsp;
Scott Pilgrim himself, Michael Cera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:59.040
and with all due respect to Groff, Cera&nbsp;
couldn't be more perfect in the role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:04.500
Described by "Vulture's" Jen Chaney as, quote,&nbsp;
"a vibe, if having no vibe counts as a vibe,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:10.140
Allan is just kind of there. Cera, a master&nbsp;
of quiet deadpan, completely nails the part.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.960
"Humans! We're fine!"
"And Allan!"

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.760
So how did Bevan and Jones settle on&nbsp;
the Kens and Barbies who ended up in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.280
the movie? When it came to the Kens, Bevan said,

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:24.240
"They were definitely really different,&nbsp;
but they had to feel like a cohesive group.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.320
You wanted to cast people who were really&nbsp;
going to get on and be great and enjoy it."

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.940
Based on the end result, it seems like that's&nbsp;
exactly what happened; "Barbie" is a film that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:36.720
even in its most emotional moments,&nbsp;
overflows with uninhibited joy.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.260
Unsurprisingly, Gerwig was also&nbsp;
closely involved in casting;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:44.610
Bevan says she showed the casting directors&nbsp;
old illustrations of classic Barbie and Ken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:49.440
dolls to narrow down a certain look, but the&nbsp;
personality was still paramount. Bevan said,

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.360
"She really wanted to get to know them as people.&nbsp;
That was very important to her. On the key Barbie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:58.020
and Ken roles, she got a very good idea of&nbsp;
what an actor was like from talking to them."

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:02.340
Here's the good news: Marvel's Simu Liu&nbsp;
really, really wanted a part in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:06.840
and he ended up playing the main nemesis&nbsp;
and eventual ally to Gosling's main Ken.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.700
"I'll beach off with you any day, Ken."

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.560
"Barbie" is in theaters now.
 

